  "instruction_steps": [
    {
      "step_number": 1,
      "instruction": "To make the filling, heat the olive oil in a pan until it starts to shimmer. Add the leek and sauté until soft but not colored. Stir in the ham with a wooden spoon, fry for 1 min, then stir in the flour and fry over a medium heat, stirring occasionally, until the mixture is golden but not burnt – this will take about 5 mins.",
      "ingredients_used": [
        "7b810455-4af3-561c-9809-a158a22326a9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "step_number": 2,
      "instruction": "Meanwhile, combine the stock and milk in a small pan and heat until steaming but not boiling. Season with a few scrapes of nutmeg. Gradually add the liquid, a few tbsp at a time, stirring constantly.",
      "ingredients_used": [
        "0ff8de47-9d66-5dc2-b74a-86cdfd8ad432",
        "050cec00-438a-5c88-a70a-e399405349fd"
      ]
    },
    {
      "step_number": 3,
      "instruction": "Once you’ve incorporated all the milk stock, continue to cook the filling over a medium heat for about 10 mins or until it thickens and leaves the sides of the pan when you stir it.",
      "timer_seconds": 600,
      "ingredients_used": [
        "0ff8de47-9d66-5dc2-b74a-86cdfd8ad432"
      ]
    },
    {
      "step_number": 4,
      "instruction": "Season with black pepper, taste and adjust the salt if necessary – the ham can be very salty to start with. The filling is now done: it has to be really thick because you don’t want the croquetas to turn into pancakes."
    },
    {
      "step_number": 5,
      "instruction": "Smooth the mixture onto a baking tray (30 x 20cm is fine). Once it has stopped steaming, cover with cling film to stop it drying out. Leave to cool before putting it in the fridge for 1 hr.",
      "timer_seconds": 3600
    },
    {
      "step_number": 6,
      "instruction": "When you're ready for the next stage, line up three bowls: the first filled with the flour, the second with beaten egg and the third with breadcrumbs. Take the ham mixture out of the fridge. Put a little bit of olive oil on your hands to make it easier to roll the croquetas.",
      "ingredients_used": [
        "52abd463-6184-5fc0-b429-1e3f28107f60",
        "bba2a77b-d154-563b-94f2-ab147475b455"
      ]
    },
    {
      "step_number": 7,
      "instruction": "Roll a spoonful of the mixture between your palms. The size and shape of the croquetas is up to you, but the easiest is a walnut-sized ball. Then begin coating as follows."
    },
    {
      "step_number": 8,
      "instruction": "Dunk the croquetas into the flour – you want a dusting – followed by the egg, then the breadcrumbs. Put them on a tray and, when you’ve used up all the mixture, place in the fridge for 30 mins.",
      "timer_seconds": 1800,
      "ingredients_used": [
        "52abd463-6184-5fc0-b429-1e3f28107f60",
        "bba2a77b-d154-563b-94f2-ab147475b455"
      ]
    },
    {
      "step_number": 9,
      "instruction": "If you have a deep-fat fryer, heat the oil to 180C and fry for a couple of mins. If not, heat the oil in a deep, heavy-bottomed saucepan until it starts to shimmer. Then add 5-6 croquetas at a time and fry until golden all over. Once cooked, drain on kitchen paper and eat straight away.",
      "temperature": {
        "value": 180,
        "unit": "celsius"
      }
    }
  ],
